# Money-Kalakshepam

> Money-Bhima! Money-Rama!  
> Money-Soma! Money-Uncle!

At the beginning of a religious discourse, Bhagavatars sing “Bala-Bhima! Balarama!” What is printed above is the song of this money-kalakshepam: money is Bhima, money is Rama, money is Soma, and money is the kinsman. Thus the supreme song of these performers is “Money-Bhima! Money-Rama!”

You may ask, “What is this, Bharata? Comrade Krishnan’s visit to Kanchipuram seems to have done serious work. He conducted a discourse, and now you conduct one too. What is special?” What I have written is not a song for the future performances of Krishnan. It is what most Bhagavatars conducting discourses on divine legends and puranas truly sing. Their devotees’ ears may receive “Bala-Bhima,” but the refrain within the performers’ minds is the one I have printed: “Money-Bhima.”

These Bhagavatars do not become sages cleansed of the three impurities and devoted to liberation. As money accumulates in their boxes, the white dhoti grows from twelve cubits and then becomes patterned silk; ordinary rudraksha seeks a gold casing; the nine-rupee ornament in a pierced ear yields its place to a thousand-rupee diamond; plain ash joins sandal paste upon the forehead; and a house acquires an upper storey and a pair of companions. The holy man who asks the Lord for immortality becomes a prosperous gentleman.

As the money increases, character slowly drains away. Spicy food, heroic speech, cosmetics concealing grey hair, and a new gait arrive. That is not my accusation. Even a buffalo rolling in mud gains some shine when someone washes it. Given grooming, even a donkey may acquire a form fit to approach a horse’s stable; doubters should observe circus donkeys. I neither marvel nor develop a fever when puranic Bhagavatars acquire polish.

They preach life’s impermanence while collecting money for medicines that will prolong their own; tell villagers the world is unstable while laying foundations strong enough for a three-storey house; sing “Seek grace, not wealth” while chasing wealth; warn others not to lose reason in the great delusion called woman while privately pursuing women; sing that anger bars the vision of Hara while becoming slaves to anger; chant that Rama’s name is sugar candy while eating richer delicacies; sing “Love the Lord’s feet” while seeking graceful women at midnight. I neither respect nor spit upon this double life of flattering and begging one’s way to prosperity. Why labour so hard merely to condemn such pettiness? When I see people prostituting their ability for a living, I knit my brow, smile faintly, and shake my head.

When a donkey brays, we may cover our ears and forgive it as its nature. But can we remain silent if the donkey declares its cry to be music? When people act according to their nature merely to fill a span-long stomach, I ordinarily let them pass. But if they boast that their conduct alone is noble and attack everyone else, how can one refrain from shooting arrows of words at those sacks of food?

These wandering men who live by moving their tongues over unbelievable puranas attack us: Self-Respect workers who stand amid stones and abuse, labouring only to raise the people; comrades of the dignity movement who abandon easier livelihoods, turn their own lives into deserts, pave a road for ordinary people, and wear villains’ accusations as ornaments of service. Imagine the anger aroused when men who sing and dance, exploit popular ignorance for money, and live allied with the evil called Aryanism condemn us. Then decide whether my words are harsh, or whether even in this burning anger I have restrained their severity.

People live by countless occupations: dancing, singing, serving masters, running, gathering, driving carts, sweeping entrances, keeping shops, and many other tasks. Some live by smiling submissively; some steal by entering backyards at midnight; some steal even through their shops. We see all this here and everywhere. But nowhere except here do people live by telling cosmic lies and making the ignorant more ignorant.

They utter naked falsehood accompanied by instruments, and ordinary people offer milk pots and money. Puranic storytellers recite tales of things nonexistent, impossible, unbelievable, irrational, and uncivilized, tales constructed in the Stone Age, and collect money from them. Such strange beings exist nowhere else. Other countries have doctors, painters, poets, bards, and religious teachers, but no villains who extract money by feeding the common people transparent lies while digging graves for their intelligence.

Western countries have no performers collecting money through discourses entitled *The Marriage of Atlas*, *The Wedding of Venus*, *The Humbling of Minerva*, or *The Coronation of Odin*. Venus, Odin, Minerva, Atlas, and other deities were once worshipped there; as knowledge dawned, people abandoned those fantasies and began dignified lives. Here, people still repeat falsehoods composed when thunder was called God, rain Maheswara, and earth a goddess, making them a means of livelihood.

Their livelihood itself is degrading and filled with deceit that seizes the country’s wealth through delusion. An intelligent society would not support people who grow their bellies through lies. They move freely in Tamil Nadu because the unlettered are numerous. Not satisfied merely to continue as long as possible and fill their stomachs, these merchants of the tongue dare to comment on public affairs and abuse reformist leaders, without knowing even the fable of the monkey that pulled the wedge.

They possess dullness as their own stock, eat what is cooked, speak whatever enters the mouth, and concentrate on collecting cash before death arrives. Yet like a dog barking at the moon or a witless man commanding the sea, they dare revile heroic people labouring to reform society. Because ordinary listeners have not matured enough to recognize their speech as naked falsehood, they give money and bow at their feet. The sellers of speech then imagine themselves great and attempt to dance like the turkey that saw the forest peacock dance. They refuse to consider the result. They will lose their present position and later yearn for what they once possessed. Their present abuse of Self-Respecters pays the advance on that outcome.

Puranic professionals condemning Self-Respecters resembles a woman living by prostitution mocking one who lives with her husband, an owl blaming a cuckoo, or a drunk criticizing a virtuous man. Sanatana orthodoxy has felt the whip; Aryanism cries beneath the lash after encountering Self-Respecters. Now these puranic men approach, mouths moving. Those who once survived by showing their stomachs now offer argument. Later, when Self-Respecters expose their qualifications to the country, they will cry that their hearts burn.

From Kirupananda Variyar with his parrot-like speech to the storyteller at a funeral rite, perhaps two hundred such people exist in Tamil Nadu. Self-Respecters ask: must the intelligence of so many people be ruined so that these two hundred may earn a living? Is that improper? They ask why the performers cannot undertake an honest occupation and why lying alone must provide their livelihood. Is that wrong?

Boundless ignorance has accumulated in the people’s minds. Progressives try to remove it, while puranic reciters drag people backward and injure society. Is it wrong to say so? You tell stories that spread blind ideas about God, stupidity in religion’s name, and cruelty in the name of commandments, mixing obscenity into the spring of people’s intelligence. Is it wrong to call that injustice? Can these puranic men prove any fault in what the Self-Respecter says?

Does criticizing them provide Self-Respecters a livelihood, money, a morning feast, or a nighttime rendezvous? What benefit or private profit do they seek or receive? Can the luxurious sellers of words answer? In the story Kamakshi marries; therefore a sari and garland are given to Meenakshi, the preacher’s wife. In the story Kakutstha is crowned; therefore the preacher receives a shower of gold. Do Self-Respecters speak for such selfish rewards? No. Their ideals are simple living, lofty thought, and joy in duty. Is it just or honest for verbal conjurers to abuse them?

Why does the Self-Respecter attack puranic men? Because they are commission agents for Aryanism, which lives by corrupting people’s minds. Let these performers adopt a new rule. Let them tell of warriors who subdued raging seas, queens of learning, the hero who engraved the tiger on the Himalayas, the brave man who liberated slaves, and the genius who advanced science. Let them earn money; we do not object. Can they not seek in our history and foreign histories the lives of such wise men, heroes, heroic women, and liberators, and use them to create clarity among the people?

They need not destroy their livelihood or the people’s reason. Where there is a will, is there no way? Why not stop narrating imaginary weddings and discourse on real scenes of love in history? Are there no stories? Have they tried? Have they even wished to try? Despite the velocity of the age, they live by speaking of fish, tortoise, and boar incarnations and of bull-riding Maheswara approaching sages’ wives with lust. Do they say anything that will give people knowledge? We ask this. Is it wrong?

A mute man sells fragrant flowers. A fool piles up filth and calls, “Come, receive it,” describing it in enchanting melody. Which will the wise take, and where will they go? Likewise, some puranic preachers are said to have Saraswati dancing upon their tongues and honey dripping from their speech. It may be true, but what is the use? Is there benefit in describing filth melodiously? Will honourable people praise a man singing Kambhoji in a liquor shop? However finely irrational stories are explained and ornamented, can intelligent people concerned for public welfare respect that course?

Periyar condemns people who live by selling such speech, and therefore these puranic men reportedly condemn him. A preacher named Kirupananda Variyar is said to have declared somewhere that Periyar is poison. Poison against what? To destroy wrongdoing? To remove injustice? For what is Variyar’s ability used?

A bat sleeping upside down on a mango tree is said to dream of tamarind. If it opened its eyes, it could see sweet mangoes. Hanging upon a tree laden with fruit, it dreams of tamarind! Variyar likewise hangs upside down upon the tree of Tamil learning while longing for the sourness of Aryanism. What use is any ability he possesses? What does it matter whether a prostitute’s eyes resemble red lotuses or not?

The wise have said scripture is a burden to the foolish, limbs a burden to those without true knowledge, the mind a burden to those without peace, and wisdom a burden to the desirous. Likewise, talent is merely a burden to puranic men. As rats gnaw and ruin the strings of a yazh, they employ their talent to corrupt Tamil minds. Variyar, one of this holy company, cannot equal in any way Self-Respecters who work for the country, awaken the people, create communal resurgence, and slowly torment Aryanism. Nor can he possess the capacity to understand the service performed by Periyar’s great army.

How can the words of countless young people, “Our Periyar is skilled at swimming against the current,” reach Variyar’s ears? He has become an attendant of Aryanism, carrying its books, touring the country and singing, becoming a disciple of wrongdoers, delighting in fools’ smiles, and living upon offerings from the intellectually blind. How can he know the greatness of Self-Respect’s service, performed without fear, favour, or indulgence? How can a man who worships the elephant-faced god, seeks the six-faced one, and lives by telling the story of Hariharaputra or praising Anandavalli’s dance understand it?

“A river, a great river, a poisonous river!” cries the man possessed by puranic madness. Yes, sir, it is a river, a mighty river that sweeps away rubbish, mounds, and forest. The river springing from Periyar’s labour pours poisonous water upon Aryanism’s roots, and Aryanism howls at the sight. I tell Variyar: do not try to cross it in your leaky boat. Take care; do not embrace danger. That great river pierces the stone dam called Gandhism and breaks through the old embankment called Brahminism. Do not fall into its whirlpool and perish; do not suffer trusting Somasundara to save you.

Mylai Iyengar entered it chest-deep and fell backward. Acharya entered to the waist and nearly fell, unable to withstand its speed. Do not set foot in it and suffer later. Recite the name of the elephant-faced god who holds fruit, appam, flattened rice, and puffed grain, and seek prosperity for your life. Periyar created a company to labour; the puranas long ago stamped out a company merely to live. Do not provoke the labouring company and later lament that your livelihood is gone.

I warn you in advance. I do not speak idly, and my words will not be wasted. All but the idle will heed them. Fix this firmly in your heart: Periyar’s soldiers work without wages or public gifts. Loud-voiced men like you receive reward, milk and fruit; women give you gifts and lovers of antiquity worship you. Yours is profitable work. Periyar’s army struggles through hardship and loss. Your company lives by praising the old age; ours lays a road for the new. Your company approaches the end of its time, while ours stands in vigorous youth.

Understand this before entering the arena. Do you labour for devotion without thought of money, like Self-Respecters? No. Your work has wages; ours asks none. Hold this mighty difference in mind. At least now try to use your tongue for good. Understand that evening approaches for puranic men. As a young woman may follow someone with sweet words without offering anything, fail to attain her desire, and return disappointed, the position of puranic preachers too will change. Let a little knowledge grow in the country, and then see whether trade by tongue continues.

Even now, to our eyes, men who pass time telling puranic tales resemble an ageing dancer attempting movements her weakened body can no longer perform and dancing without delight or sense. Recognize this and confine yourself to the “devotional” path of praying to God to pass this world without trouble and paying an advance for the next. Do not collide with the power of Self-Respect. It is a river rushing from a mountain that has already broken many skulls filled with conceit. This I tell Variyar.

As a result of this money-kalakshepam, the course of religious storytellers may change. If it does not, their course will rot; I cannot prevent it. The lash wielded by the warrior called Time will make them tremble and cry.

16-7-1944

## That Picture!

Soviet forces, driving the retreating Nazis before them, have now forced them to flee and hide within Poland, Czechoslovakia, Finland, and Estonia. The victorious blows of the heroic Soviet army are today music to all who cherish democracy. It has not merely recaptured Soviet forts from the Germans, recovered strategically important cities and ports, and liberated productive regions; it is driving the Nazi army back into Germany itself.

At the same time, Allied forces advance in Normandy after crossing many critical stages and breaking Rommel’s fox-like schemes. The same occurs in Italy. Japan too meets defeat after defeat on sea and land.

The Soviet army will not stop there. New forces are reportedly leaving Moscow to enter Germany. They advance toward Berlin, where only two years ago Soviet people were mocked; where communism was ridiculed in beer halls and dance rooms; where Nazi propaganda proclaimed that Kiev was captured, Kharkov had fallen, Stalingrad was shattered, and Minsk was theirs; where Hitler roared that he would plant the swastika in Moscow within three months.

They advance toward the Berlin where arrogant German generals, delighted by the glitter of Hitler’s new decorations, claimed that the Soviet land had collapsed, Stalin had gone into hiding, Moscow was empty, and the Russian government had fled to Kuibyshev; where clothes and medals stripped from Soviet soldiers who fell fighting heroically were made playthings; where food, clothing, and other goods looted from the homes of Soviet women who had lost sons, husbands, elder brothers, and younger brothers in fallen Russian cities were proudly distributed; and from which countless aircraft, enormous tanks, and cannon departed to shatter Soviet fortresses.

Into that Berlin, where Adolf Hitler strutted and terrified the world under titles such as German dictator, hero of Norway, master of France, protector of Denmark and Luxembourg, lord of Poland, benefactor of Finland and Czechoslovakia, patriarch of Italy, guardian of the sea, king of the sky, ally of Japan, and man sent by God to rule the world, Soviet forces from Moscow will enter next month proclaiming, “The Nazi has fallen; the destroyer of the world is finished!”

From Moscow, where Stalin rules with diplomatic skill and battlefield courage; from Moscow where Lenin raised his voice; from Moscow which established the new law that the earth belongs to those who labour; from the fortress of heroes who declared that even if life were lost they would never surrender honour, who saw corpses and ruined families and Nazi forces rushing like wildfire yet neither lost heart nor submitted, but fought beside the pit of death, waded through blood, made bridges of bodies, seized victory by the ear, and kissed it, the Soviet army will enter Berlin.

What an astonishing, hair-raising reversal! The Nazis’ dream of flying the swastika over Moscow has collapsed. The army departing Moscow will tear to pieces the swastika flying in Berlin. The world should not merely hear and rejoice at this event, worthy of first place in history; it should see it.

Soviet cinematographers are therefore arranging to film the Soviet army entering Germany. Merely imagining the joy and pride of those camera operators fills us with joy. They who once filmed a lover giving a flower and kissing the lips of a bright-eyed woman in a decorative garden will now film Moscow’s army entering Berlin, the communist hammer falling upon the fascist camp. They are worthy of honour. The world waits eagerly to see the picture. Every brave person in the world will surely claim it as his or her own film.

16-7-1944

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim in the bilingual document. The main title is a wordplay on `காலட்சேபம்` (*kalakshepam*, a religious storytelling performance), reshaped around `காசு`/money and the refrain `பணபீமா, பணராமா`; “Money-Kalakshepam” preserves the function rather than the exact sound. The canonical scan bundle contains the separately titled short piece `அந்தப் படம்!` immediately after the first dated essay; both pieces and both printed dates are retained in their original order. `கருபானந்தவாரியார்` is normalized in English as Kirupananda Variyar. Some wartime place names in the OCR are damaged; standard English forms are used where identification is clear. The source’s invective and stigmatizing comparisons are translated as historical polemic without endorsement.
